{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789321382,"nonce":"ThMAGm6YcOqU2vfsXkoxPKaFPqM=","title":"Python: a value of the wrong type read out of a box was garbage, not a TypeError","message":"xs = [0] * 3\n    xs[0] = \"a\"\n    print(xs)              # [416611827713, 0, 0]\n\n    def f(x: int) -\u003e int:\n        return x + 1\n    print(f([\"s\", 2][0]))  # 493921239042\n\n    xs = [1, 2]\n    xs[0] = 2.5            # [2, 2]\n\n`coerce(Object -\u003e Int/Float/Str/Bool)` was the compiler's `Cast`, which reads the box's payload by size with no tag check, so a str box read as an int gave its pointer bits; `coerce(Float -\u003e Int)` truncated; and two unrelated types went through a box and the same unchecked read. Also `zb_any_setitem` refused every typed list (\"'list' object does not support item assignment\", so `grid[i][j] = v` on a list of int rows failed), `zb_any_getitem` copied a typed list whole on every read, and `coerce(List(Object) -\u003e List(Str))` was a reinterpreting cast (`\"\".join(parts)` on an appended-to list printed nothing).\n\nNow: `zb_any_as_i64/f64/str/bool` are checked reads raising TypeError with CPython's wording; `coerce` uses them for every dynamic-to-primitive read and for any pair of types with no conversion between them, and guards them with the pending check where the lowerer drains hoisted statements (`Lowerer.guards`, off in hand-built functions whose callers check). Storage the lowering itself wrote (cells, globals, generator environments, fields) is read back unchecked (`Lowerer::trusted`). `zb_any_getitem`/`setitem` read and write typed lists in place; `zb_list_from_any_\u003ckind\u003e` converts a dynamic list element by element (`zb_hook_unbox_instance(x, tag)` for instance lists); `round(x, n)` on an int keeps the int; `builtin_arms` emits the receiver's hoisted statements ahead of its `let`.\n\nWhere CPython would widen the list (`xs[0] = 2.5` into a list of ints) this now raises TypeError instead of producing garbage. The conformant answer is list element inference across writes and aliases, tracked separately.","files":null}]}